  "worked_solution": "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+9y+4.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false.  We have that $f(1)=(1)^{3}+6(1)^{2}+9(1)+4=20\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  We have that $f(-4)=(-4)^{3}+6(-4)^{2}+9(-4)+4=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-4$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+4)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\u2193$ | $-4$ | $-8$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+4)({y}^{2}+2y+1) \\\\  & =(y+4)(y+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has $2$ distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=-4,\\,{y}_{2}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
